WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.128 --> 00:00:03.878
(introductory hip-hop music)

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.120
- Well welcome back to another
episode of the Addy Hour.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.850
It's my pleasure to continue
to host these conversations.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.830
I've definitely been
appreciative of the engagement

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.480
of all of you as listeners,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.280
and today I'm very excited to be able

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.280
to host a conversation
on justice, activism,

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.060
and the church with
Rev. Dr. Otis Moss, III.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.110
Dr. Moss, thanks so much

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.910
for being here on the Addy Hour podcast.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.260
- It is my pleasure.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.940
Thank you for having me.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:30.930
- Of course.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.990
And we have had the opportunity
to interact previously.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.250
Dr. Moss and I were part

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.600
of a provocative panel back
in September of '22 entitled,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.150
Is Christianity Bad
For Your Mental Health?

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.070
So you can just imagine
some of the conversations

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.660
that came up there,

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.780
but really excited to be able
to interact again in this way.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.280
And just by way of introduction,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.590
for those who may not be
familiar with Dr. Moss,

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.050
want to just let you know
that he is the senior pastor

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:58.320
of Trinity United Church of
Christ in Chicago, Illinois.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.590
He is a preacher, an
activist, and an author.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.300
And also, over the last two decades,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.480
he's someone who has practiced
and preached a Black theology

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.870
that unapologetically calls
attentions to the problems

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.930
of mass incarceration,
environmental justice,

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.450
and economic inequality.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.730
He is a native of Cleveland, Ohio,

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.860
an honors graduate of Morehouse College.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.810
He also has a master's of divinity

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.100
from the Yale Divinity School,

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.840
so home turf in a sense for me,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.790
and a doctorate of ministry

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.070
from Chicago Theological Seminary.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.250
He is currently a professor in
the McAfee School of Theology

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.230
at Mercer University.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.960
And he is someone who is
a third-generation warrior

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.090
for civil and human rights.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.820
He's committed to preaching
a prophetic message of love

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.540
and justice, which he believes
are inseparable companions

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.470
that form the foundation of
the gospel of Jesus Christ.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.780
I should also mention,
this is a legacy for him,

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.790
both personally and in
terms of his family.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:57.210
He's been heavily influenced
and has a foundation

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.550
from his father, Dr. Otis Moss Jr.,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.980
and also from Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.370
whom his father worked with at
one point as well, so again,

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.140
just to give you all just
a little bit of insight,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.990
I know we'll hear more but
just appreciate the work

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.210
that you continue to do and
your willingness to be here

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.010
for this podcast conversation.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.260
- Thank you, I'm honored.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.183
- Excellent.

00:02:17.183 --> 00:02:18.420
And as my listeners know,

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.180
one of the benefits of these
conversations is we have a lot

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.940
of guests who are just
generous with their openness

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.730
and being willing to peel
back the layers as it were.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.020
So I wanted to just do a check-in

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.450
and see how you're doing
at this point in time.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.700
With everything that we've
experienced as a society

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.470
and especially, soberingly,

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.140
with just all of the
challenges around gun violence,

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.180
both the mass shootings
that seem to be happening

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.510
on a weekly basis, and all
of the other gun violence

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.360
that does not get that same
national attention, so just,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.490
if you're willing to share how
you're doing at this point.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.260
- I appreciate that.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.940
Right now, I am, I use the
word okay, when you say,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.753
you know, scale of one to 10,
that usually means like five.

00:02:58.753 --> 00:03:01.050
(both laugh)

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.313
So I'm in the middle.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.230
I received a call that one

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:12.230
of our deacons actually
found his younger brother

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:18.950
who had committed suicide
by hanging unfortunately.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.573
- Oh, wow.

00:03:20.573 --> 00:03:22.140
- And he found the body

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:27.120
and just a, just devastating.
- Oh my goodness.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.613
- And right before
getting on this podcast,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.750
one of our members just
sent us a text that she went

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:40.160
to the hospital to see her father, she,

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.170
her father was at assisted living place,

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.230
but they never called her to share

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.720
that he was being
transferred to the hospital.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.310
She gets to the hospital,
no one had called her,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.920
no one said anything,
and her father's gone.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:53.753
- Wow.

00:03:53.753 --> 00:03:58.740
- And you know, just, you
know, the weight of, you know,

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:02.500
those two people that walking with them

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.660
as they are preparing
for home goings, both in,

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:17.180
one, how should I say it, one
who had lived a beautiful,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:24.950
long life, and then one where, you know,

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:30.783
you have a family that's
with, as it is with suicide,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.420
you raise questions and
trying to walk with the family

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.740
and let them know that
this is not punishment,

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.740
your loved one is loved by God
and God's arm, all of that,

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.640
without all of the noise
and trash that seep

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.980
into our subconscious

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:57.980
because we had such horrific pastoral care

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:04.110
and just bad theology and just bad public,

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:08.190
civic conversation about people

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:13.190
who just really are looking
for individuals to support them

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.050
and to walk with them and love them.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:17.716
- Exactly, well--
- So I'm at a five.

00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:20.340
I'm at a five, I'm at a five,
just doing what, you know,

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.857
what you do in the Black
churches, you know,

00:05:23.857 --> 00:05:25.680
trying to, you know, to walk with people

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.810
in their most vulnerable moments.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:28.643
- Yeah.

00:05:28.643 --> 00:05:30.090
Well definitely appreciate your level

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:31.380
of honesty just in talking

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:32.880
about where you are at this point in time,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.740
and I know that a lot
of listeners, you know,

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.330
especially those who have experience

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.790
or are in the Black church can
relate to that piece as well,

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.610
but some listeners might
also even feel surprised

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:43.740
to hear you say that you're at a five

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.120
with that level of heaviness

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.550
that you often have to walk through.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.770
But you know, you touched
on so many important pieces

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:51.300
in terms of meeting
people where they're at

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.810
but then just all of the
noise that can come as well.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.730
Just to maybe even peel
back a little bit more

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:58.890
even before we jump into
some of the other topics,

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:03.360
how do you as a pastor and
a faith leader navigate that

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.840
for those that you are
ministrating to with all the noise,

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:07.920
trying to help them navigate

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:10.770
but then also trying to
make sure that it's framed

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.560
in the right way, both for
them, for their families,

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.060
for the church, for society?

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:18.120
I know that's a very large
question, but just curious,

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.500
anything you wanna elaborate.
- Well I think

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:20.670
on a personal level,

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:25.670
it is important that you
are grounded in community,

00:06:25.987 --> 00:06:27.870
you know, your own community.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:30.300
I'm not talking about, quote unquote,

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.400
just the church community
because for those

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.410
who are pastors who're
probably listening to,

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:38.250
the most difficult thing is
pastors need to be pastored,

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:42.550
pastors need to have a community
in which they are nurtured

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.940
and cared, so that they
can be good caregivers,

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:52.860
especially during moments of
vulnerability and challenge.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:55.830
And so being in a community,

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.437
there's your family and
then there are people

00:06:58.437 --> 00:07:00.750
that're around you that support you,

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:03.150
so that's one of the things,

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:07.717
and then having the
appropriate spiritual practices

00:07:07.717 --> 00:07:12.717
for yourself, you know, the
time of silence, you know,

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.093
mindfulness, prayer,

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:18.603
music for me has always been very helpful.

00:07:20.667 --> 00:07:23.400
And as a spiritual practice,

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:28.400
listening to and spending
time just listening to music

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:33.393
that puts you in a particular
place, and reading.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.570
Reading scripture,

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:41.570
I'm going through a book
right now called "The Teaching

00:07:42.457 --> 00:07:45.270
"of Christian Mystics," basically,

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:48.453
and so what I do is I read out loud,

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.140
because reading out loud
changes things, number one.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:56.400
Number two, most of what we read

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.510
that is ancient was never
meant to be read in your head.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:05.400
This is oral transmission,
so why do we become so modern

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.930
and say, "Oh, I'll read this in my head,"

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:11.930
but when you read out loud,
all of a sudden the phrasing,

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:18.290
the voice, the pause catches you

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.780
in a different way than
when you read something

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:22.923
in your head.

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:26.880
I mean, I'm encouraging,

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.987
I teach and I encourage
my students, I say,

00:08:28.987 --> 00:08:32.647
"Before you even go to a scripture

00:08:32.647 --> 00:08:34.717
"and you're gonna start
trying to work on a sermon,

00:08:34.717 --> 00:08:37.177
"I want you to read it out
loud and then read it out loud

00:08:37.177 --> 00:08:39.847
"in five different translations, you know,

00:08:39.847 --> 00:08:41.910
"and watch what happens."

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:45.450
Just by reading, you're not
trying to force anything,

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:50.250
or, just read it and it becomes
a great meditative process

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:55.250
and discipline to read out loud,

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.140
and to read because,

00:08:58.140 --> 00:08:59.970
for example, I'm sorry
I'm talking so long,

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:00.957
I know it's about my job,
- No, it's good. (laughs)

00:09:00.957 --> 00:09:04.560
- but, like if you say
the Lord is my shepherd,

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.560
or do you say, the Lord is my shepherd?

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:12.453
Or do you say, the Lord is my shepherd?

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:15.723
Now, all the exact same words,

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.800
but based on the usage of your voice,

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.410
a different meaning comes forth,

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.620
and different emotion is presented.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:33.620
That's why reading out loud,
reading poetry out loud,

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.860
reading scripture out loud,

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:41.424
and that's why we sing songs out loud.

00:09:41.424 --> 00:09:43.200
(both laugh)

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:44.033
- Yep.

00:09:44.033 --> 00:09:45.540
- Like songs in my head,
no, you start, you know,

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:46.373
start singing it.

00:09:46.373 --> 00:09:50.250
You know, I mean, it's one
thing to say it in your head

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:54.120
but out, you know, singing
it out, thinking, you know,

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:57.840
becomes a powerful art form.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.060
- Mm, well, that's so
powerful and so good.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:01.140
I know people are listening to that

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:02.250
and even just reflecting.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:03.570
I mean, even just that simple phrase

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:05.430
that you had in three different ways,

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:07.050
just the power that comes from that.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:08.430
And I mean, you also
talked about community

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:10.980
and then maybe we're
getting off script here too,

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.560
but how do you see those
two pieces going together?

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:16.503
Especially as a pastor, I
mean, with things that happen,

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:19.203
not just in our congregations
- You know, we're--

00:10:19.203 --> 00:10:22.230
- but in the world and as we
navigate them as communities?

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:26.105
- I think community's necessary
for human flourishing.

00:10:26.105 --> 00:10:30.390
We're designed as community creatures.

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:35.390
You know, we were created in
community to be in community.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:40.590
Genesis talks about humanity being alone.

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:42.000
It's not good to be alone.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:43.461
There are times you need to be alone.

00:10:43.461 --> 00:10:46.026
(laughs) Lemme add that.

00:10:46.026 --> 00:10:51.026
But we learn in community,
we are nurtured in community,

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:58.580
we develop in community, and
when we experience trauma,

00:10:59.081 --> 00:11:02.470
we come out of trauma

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.360
or learn how to navigate trauma
through healthy community.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:12.900
And without a healthy community, it's not,

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:16.800
you are not able to
flourish as a human being,

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:21.210
and you're not able to
reach your full potential.

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:25.260
You need to be in a healthy community,

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:29.913
and when families are unhealthy,
families are community,

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:34.710
it takes a toll on your soul,

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:39.710
and you have to place yourself in a space

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:43.560
where you can be in and
around healthy people,

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:47.887
and that's one of the
aspects of the Black church.

00:11:47.887 --> 00:11:52.887
And I say the Black church
specifically, that they're,

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.950
in a church there are
multiple communities,

00:11:55.950 --> 00:12:00.950
and when you find the space
of the appropriate community,

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:06.260
an intergenerational community
of elders and younger people,

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:07.440
people who are middle-aged,

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:11.370
some who are married and some who are not

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:16.370
and you have this potpourri
of people in your garden,

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:24.830
are providing aspects for human
development, and you know,

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.180
it's kinda like, just
like a regular garden.

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:32.760
You know, some people are like basil,

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.253
some people are like mint.

00:12:34.253 --> 00:12:35.086
(both laugh)

00:12:35.086 --> 00:12:38.040
So you know, you know, some
people are like rose bushes

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:41.337
but got a whole lotta thorns,
you know, so you know,

00:12:41.337 --> 00:12:43.829
and you have to know that
they're not all the same

00:12:43.829 --> 00:12:48.829
and together, they give all
of the necessary nutrients

00:12:51.481 --> 00:12:54.873
for a person to truly flourish.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:57.300
- That's so wonderful
to hear and you know,

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.000
my listeners have heard
me talk about community

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.470
in lots of different settings,

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:04.320
often bringing up just the
biology of what that does

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:05.730
in our brains and the social binds,

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.490
so it's so nice to hear
from a holistic perspective,

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:09.810
just to hear you actually emphasize

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:11.400
and talk about community
within the church,

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:12.840
how important that is for our soul,

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:15.150
and all these different pieces together

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:17.460
and how that really helps us navigate,

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:19.500
and even as we've talked
about things like traumas

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:22.320
as you hinted at, and how people
walk through that together

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:23.460
as a community,

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:25.710
and just the ways that God has designed us

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:26.550
to be in community,

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.160
the ways we can see that show
up in our biological changes,

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:32.070
the way we can see that in
psychological processes,

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:34.580
the way we can see that on
the global scale, and again,

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:36.360
it just seems, in so many ways,

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:39.450
that the heart of what you
are doing in your roles,

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:41.790
even as I think about
what you've talked about

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:43.560
in terms of mental health but also some

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:45.270
of the social justice work,

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:47.880
thinking about criminal injustices,

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.940
just the activism and the
family history that you have,

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.340
how does that aspect of community for you,

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.980
what does that actually
look like across all

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.660
of these different spectrums?

00:13:57.660 --> 00:13:59.280
Because again, people, you know,

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:00.300
might be hearing about and saying,

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.760
well you're touching on so
many different components,

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:04.893
and how do you tie those all together?

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:07.973
- Hm, that's an interesting question.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:10.650
How do we tie them all together?

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:15.650
Well for me, I was raised in a space

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:21.020
that believed that the
Black community is varied

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:29.150
and diverse, and it is also
your responsibility not only

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:32.670
to be a part of,

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:36.393
but to ensure that this
community is thriving.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.310
So be a part and thriving,
so there's, you know,

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:44.460
there's kinda the church,
you know, community,

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:47.940
there's what I would call,
like for those who've gone

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:49.710
to HBCUs, you know,

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:51.840
there's another community
that develops off of that.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.850
If you went to a PWI,

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:57.360
you've created your own little
community on that level.

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:01.350
Then there're the community
that you have at your work.

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:02.820
Then there's the family community,

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:06.060
then there's the community of friends,

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:11.060
friends that you've developed
as a child, grew up with,

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:15.160
friends that you have connected with

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:18.873
during your adulting process which is,

00:15:18.873 --> 00:15:21.540
some is called college,
or if you are, you know,

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:24.270
you're in apprentice program,
or whatever it may be,

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:29.270
or the military, and then
there are friends that develop

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:33.930
as a result of common trauma

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:37.923
and tragedy that you end up
developing relationships with,

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:41.356
and then there are the people that you,

00:15:41.356 --> 00:15:44.970
a person that you choose
to be your life partner,

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:48.330
that I would hope would be
your best friend, you know,

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:50.040
that you should go down that road.

00:15:52.817 --> 00:15:57.817
All of those communities make
the fullness of who we are.

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:05.953
And all of those communities
have their North Star

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:17.420
and also have moments where
the stars are obscured

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:20.580
and they get off course.

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:21.930
All of those communities.

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:26.790
That's just a part, if you
dare to claim to be human

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:28.950
and you seek to live your call,

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:33.090
you will deal with those issues.

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:34.860
But all these aspects

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:37.710
of community though make up who you are

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:42.710
and make up who we see
as our wider community.

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:46.638
And I'm, you know, speaking, you know,

00:16:46.638 --> 00:16:48.120
from the perspective of someone

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:50.430
who is out of the African
American community,

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:53.940
and there're some very unique
aspects to our community,

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:56.700
I mean, just like barber
shop community, you know?

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.220
I mean that, I like
going to the barber shop

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:03.450
and that community of
people, usually brothers,

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:04.590
especially when they, you know,

00:17:04.590 --> 00:17:07.500
start arguing about sports and start lying

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:08.333
about other stuff,

00:17:08.333 --> 00:17:12.330
there's something that's
very endearing about being

00:17:12.330 --> 00:17:17.010
in that space and there's
something stress-relieving

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:19.500
about being in that space
as someone, you know,

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:22.440
they're cutting your hair,
you know, you get the hookup,

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:25.072
you know, with the cut and
whatnot and you get your,

00:17:25.072 --> 00:17:27.480
you know, to argue or hear a whole bunch

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:31.503
of ridiculous theories about the world,

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.100
there's no other space
where people get a chance

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:39.810
to communicate, lie, promote.

00:17:42.389 --> 00:17:44.070
(both laugh)

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:44.970
- It's all there.

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:49.203
- It's everything, it's
everything in those spaces.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:53.010
You know, it's similar to when, you know,

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.110
people gather together
and they play Spades

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:56.310
or something of that nature,

00:17:56.310 --> 00:17:58.530
you have that level of energy sometimes,

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:01.080
but you know, comes about.

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:01.913
- Yeah.

00:18:01.913 --> 00:18:04.050
And some surprises that aren't surprises

00:18:04.050 --> 00:18:06.030
in terms of the things that
come out of people's mouths,

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:07.116
but it's healthy too.
- Oh my gosh.

00:18:07.116 --> 00:18:08.640
(Nii laughs)
Yeah, yeah, it is.

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:10.320
It's fascinating, it's just,

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:15.320
it's the best comedy in the
world is in the barber shop,

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:19.320
because you have some
of the best storytellers

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:23.250
and they're telling stories
that are absolutely not true

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:27.270
and everybody knows it, but we
still want to hear the story,

00:18:27.270 --> 00:18:29.340
no matter what, because they're
such a good storyteller.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:30.930
- Yep, yep.

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:34.004
I mean even, not to make it
too academic all of a sudden,

00:18:34.004 --> 00:18:34.837
but I know there's a lot

00:18:34.837 --> 00:18:37.320
of initiatives just even using that space

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:40.140
and all the benefits of it to
actually help people open up

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:42.390
about mental health challenges,
because that's a lot of,

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:44.340
that's the first point of
contact for a lot of people,

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:46.380
so that, be able to be
equipped in that way

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:48.000
to notice something,

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:49.440
to touch on it in the space

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:50.700
where there's already that sense

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:53.070
of community has been really important

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:54.030
in a lot of communities just

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:56.040
for really pushing that
conversation forward.

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:58.770
- Yeah, Chicago, we have
a couple members here

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:01.390
who were part of public health initiatives

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:05.220
where they were using the barber shop

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:07.800
for the public health
initiative for, you know,

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:09.900
people getting checked,
getting tested, all of that,

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:12.180
but also for mental health check-in,

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:17.180
and now there is an initiative
around barber shop books,

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:23.240
placing in barber shops,
books for young people.

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:27.513
They can take them, you know,
there's no cost to them.

00:19:27.513 --> 00:19:28.860
You know, they say, "Hey,
you can take and," you know,

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:31.082
it's kinda like read and return,

00:19:31.082 --> 00:19:35.850
and so there's been this
new movement around literacy

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:38.760
in connection to barber shops.

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:41.070
Literacy and mental health I think

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:44.700
also go directly together.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:47.880
You give people spaces
where they're able to read

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:50.610
and to imagine and to rethink,

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:53.160
and to see themselves in spaces

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:56.970
that they never would
normally think of themselves,

00:19:56.970 --> 00:20:00.416
and that's one of the powers
I think of Afrofuturism,

00:20:00.416 --> 00:20:05.416
the idea of seeing Black people
in a variety of ways, not,

00:20:08.779 --> 00:20:10.110
I read an article years ago,

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:14.250
it was called "The Documentary
Impulse in Black Film,"

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:16.680
and you know, I was kinda
like, what is this article?

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:17.513
What is this about?

00:20:17.513 --> 00:20:19.560
And I started reading, I
was like, oh, I get it.

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:24.330
The writer was saying
that for everybody else,

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:27.330
when we write something
or something is presented,

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:29.700
we get to say it's fiction.

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:31.470
But for Black people,

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:34.290
no matter even (chuckles) if
it's just run of the mill,

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:35.123
it's like, oh yeah,

00:20:35.123 --> 00:20:36.420
this is really like a documentary

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:38.670
because this is the way Black people are.

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:41.103
We don't even get to play.

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:45.270
We don't even get to fantasize,

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:48.603
because we're always told,
no, it has to be real.

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:53.430
And that's the beauty of
Afrofuturism is that it allows us

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:55.950
to expand our imagination

00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:57.210
and one of the most dangerous things

00:20:57.210 --> 00:20:58.707
is to have a colonized imagination

00:20:58.707 --> 00:21:00.577
that limits your ability to be able

00:21:00.577 --> 00:21:04.080
to witness what you can be or
what your community can be.

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:04.913
- Yeah.

00:21:04.913 --> 00:21:06.330
Well that's so, so important.

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:07.170
I'm glad your bringing that up,

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:08.880
and just reminds me of a lot of the work

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:10.260
that you're already doing,

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:13.170
just in terms of Afrofuturism
but then also the power

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:15.510
of community to change narratives, I mean,

00:21:15.510 --> 00:21:17.190
as you were talking about with literacy

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:18.690
as with mental health.

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:20.700
And I know you as a church have done a lot

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:22.170
of work around that,

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:24.690
even from individuals who
were formerly incarcerated,

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:27.510
and how you've been changing
the narrative there as well.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:29.370
So I'd be curious if you'd
just be willing to talk

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:31.340
about that a little bit
for our listeners to know,

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:33.300
to hear about some of
those innovative things

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:37.140
that you're doing to really
shift perspective, expectation,

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:38.790
and opportunity.

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:41.610
- Well, one of the things that we, well,

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:43.710
let me just begin this way,

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:45.510
that there are three pillars

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:49.890
to any type of community
development work we do here

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:51.900
at Trinity. We've done a lot
of community development work,

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:52.980
and I'll explain a little bit more

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:55.080
about community development work.

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:59.430
But the three pillars
are economic development,

00:21:59.430 --> 00:22:02.580
that in other words, our
community must benefit.

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:06.150
And the second pillar is
that it must be sustainable,

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:07.803
it must be green.

00:22:09.387 --> 00:22:12.090
And so there must be some sustainability,

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:13.800
must be a green initiative.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:15.150
And then the third pillar is,

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:18.870
it must disrupt the mass
incarceration system.

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:20.850
So it has to have those reasons,

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:24.300
we've gotta benefit from
it, it's gotta be green,

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:26.220
and it's got to disrupt,

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:29.910
so I like to say that
it's gotta be blessed

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:32.850
by Black folk and Black
folk gotta be blessed by it.

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:35.610
It's gotta be green in terms of things

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:38.700
that we're gonna flow into
our pockets and to our bank,

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:42.540
and it's also got to bind
the wounds of the blood

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:46.260
that's been shed as a result
of mass incarceration,

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:48.918
so I'd like to say it's red,
black, and green, you know,

00:22:48.918 --> 00:22:51.495
to borrow from Marcus Garvey.

00:22:51.495 --> 00:22:52.830
(both chuckle)

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:56.520
But what we did with one
of first initiatives,

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:59.760
we renovated our church and
we renovated the church green.

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:00.593
Green statement,

00:23:00.593 --> 00:23:03.183
we wanted to make a
LEED-certified building.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:08.880
In doing that, we said, one,
the contractors must be Black.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:13.110
Two, those working on this
building must be people

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:16.140
who come from the mass
incarceration system.

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:18.870
Three, it has to be green.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:23.670
And this was kind of a challenge

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:27.510
because most people who were bidding

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:29.220
and things of that nature,
"We've never done this before,"

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:30.337
was like, "Well you can work with us,

00:23:30.337 --> 00:23:32.207
"this is the way it works."

00:23:32.207 --> 00:23:36.270
We are the primary contract,
you're a subcontractor,

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:38.340
so this is the way we're gonna do this.

00:23:39.797 --> 00:23:43.440
And so we created and built, you know,

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:45.510
renovated our church in that manner.

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:47.010
Then at one of our next projects,

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:49.800
there's a library
literally down the street,

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:51.420
walking distance from our church,

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:54.120
has the largest collection of
African American literature

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:54.953
in the Midwest.

00:23:54.953 --> 00:23:57.480
It's called the Carter G. Woodson Library.

00:23:57.480 --> 00:23:58.950
The Vivian Harsh collection,

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:01.380
I mean they have letters of DuBois sent

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:03.510
to people in Chicago,

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:06.330
I mean it's primary resources,
if you're doing your PhD,

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:08.910
University of Chicago,
University of Illinois, DePaul,

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:10.904
you have to use this library,
but it's in the hood.

00:24:10.904 --> 00:24:12.272
It's in our hood.

00:24:12.272 --> 00:24:13.927
We have a great hood, by the way.

00:24:15.523 --> 00:24:19.260
And so the building had
scaffolding around it,

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:21.240
not because the city was doing work,

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:23.310
but the original contractor did a poor job

00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:24.720
and they were worried that
the bricks were gonna fall

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:26.210
on the heads of people
walking into the library.

00:24:26.210 --> 00:24:27.510
- (blows out) Wow.

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:32.340
- And the scaffolding had
been out there for 20 years,

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:34.170
so people got used to it.

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:35.003
- Wow.

00:24:35.003 --> 00:24:36.660
Community development corporation
just raised the question,

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:37.987
was like, "Why is there
scaffolding outside

00:24:37.987 --> 00:24:38.820
"of the building?"

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:40.200
No one had raised the
question, it's like, well,

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:42.180
that's when we found the story,

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:45.087
and we organized with the community,

00:24:45.087 --> 00:24:46.830
and when I say organized
with the community,

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:49.890
other community partners, other
organizations, block clubs,

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:51.630
you name it, and we found out

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:54.120
that the library's heavily
used, people love it,

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:56.460
but it is falling apart.

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:58.057
So we were going to the city and saying,

00:24:58.057 --> 00:25:03.057
"Hey, we want you to
renovate the library."

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:04.560
And we already did our research.

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:07.830
There were five other libraries
that were either built new

00:25:07.830 --> 00:25:12.210
from the ground up or renovated,
million-dollar renovations,

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:16.230
like five, 10, 15, and
$20 million renovations,

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:18.937
and they came back to us, they said,

00:25:18.937 --> 00:25:20.520
"Oh, we don't have the money."

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:21.397
I said, "Well, you had the money

00:25:21.397 --> 00:25:22.935
"in every white neighborhood."

00:25:22.935 --> 00:25:24.217
(both laugh)

00:25:24.217 --> 00:25:26.820
"But you didn't have the money
in the Black neighborhood."

00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:29.282
So after this initial meeting,

00:25:29.282 --> 00:25:30.115
at the very end

00:25:30.115 --> 00:25:32.400
of the meeting I just
simply asked one question.

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:33.997
I said, "When's your next library meeting,

00:25:33.997 --> 00:25:35.550
"the library commission meeting?"

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:37.440
And like, oh, they meet
once a month on like,

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:39.060
it was Wednesday I think.

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:40.740
And I said, "Thank you very much."

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:45.740
And unbeknownst to them, what
I did was I, the next Sunday,

00:25:46.290 --> 00:25:49.890
I got up in the pulpit and I
shared with the congregation,

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:51.397
you know, what had happened, and I said,

00:25:51.397 --> 00:25:53.497
"There's a library
commission meeting coming up

00:25:53.497 --> 00:25:55.207
"on said date.

00:25:55.207 --> 00:26:00.127
"We'd love it if you would show
up to express the importance

00:26:00.127 --> 00:26:02.460
"of the library in the community."

00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:07.140
Now, one must understand that
library commission meetings,

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:11.070
nobody goes to, unless you're
into the Dewey Decimal System.

00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:13.320
No one goes to a library
commission meeting.

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:16.260
So on this library commission
meeting was so perfunctory,

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:17.910
they're boring, you know,

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:20.163
they go on for all of about
50 minutes and they, you know,

00:26:20.163 --> 00:26:22.410
they go away, they said hey, you know,

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:24.570
we're gonna hire another
librarian or something.

00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:28.020
And this library commission meeting,

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:29.943
all of these Black people show up.

00:26:31.032 --> 00:26:34.080
I mean, and everybody
has the same question.

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:34.951
I wasn't at the meeting.

00:26:34.951 --> 00:26:36.870
(Nii laughs)

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:39.397
They're asking this, "So you
built five other libraries,

00:26:39.397 --> 00:26:44.070
"but you don't have the money
for the Carter G. Woodson?"

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:46.113
Please let that name sit.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:51.660
Named after one of the greatest
historian intellectuals.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:53.490
The reason that we have
Black History Month,

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:54.900
a Black History Week that was started

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:56.887
by Carter G. Woodson, you know,

00:26:56.887 --> 00:27:00.270
"The Mis-Education of the
Negro," Carter G. Woodson,

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:02.520
and so the meeting ended at like,

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:06.630
let's say 7:59 I think it
was, I got a call at 8:01.

00:27:06.630 --> 00:27:07.463
- Wow.

00:27:07.463 --> 00:27:09.097
- And they were saying,
"Can we meet, you know,

00:27:09.097 --> 00:27:10.249
"as soon as possible?"

00:27:10.249 --> 00:27:13.380
I was like, "Sure, I'd be more
than happy to meet with you."

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:16.500
And so our team met with
the library commission

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:18.757
and they walk in, they
were smiling and they said,

00:27:18.757 --> 00:27:21.847
"We want you to know,
we found $10 million.

00:27:21.847 --> 00:27:24.030
"We got a $10 million grant,"
- That was quick,

00:27:24.030 --> 00:27:25.080
that was quick. (laughs)
- It was that quick,

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:28.087
was that really quick,
"to rebuild the library,

00:27:28.087 --> 00:27:28.920
"to renovate the library."

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:31.027
I was like, "Oh, that's really wonderful."

00:27:31.027 --> 00:27:32.197
"So I want you to know that all

00:27:32.197 --> 00:27:34.500
"of your concerns have
been taken care of."

00:27:34.500 --> 00:27:36.060
We said, "No."

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:38.827
I said, "First of all, will
Black people benefit from it?

00:27:38.827 --> 00:27:40.717
"Who will be the contractors?

00:27:40.717 --> 00:27:44.587
"Second thing is, will
it be a green design?

00:27:44.587 --> 00:27:47.137
"Third, will you use brothers and sisters

00:27:47.137 --> 00:27:49.950
"from the mass incarceration
system to do the work?"

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:52.117
And they looked dumbfounded and said,

00:27:52.117 --> 00:27:56.197
"Well, there's no policy to do that.

00:27:56.197 --> 00:27:57.782
"We've never done
anything like that before.

00:27:57.782 --> 00:27:59.437
"That would cost a lot.

00:27:59.437 --> 00:28:01.470
"That would," you know,
they were just using cost

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:03.060
as an excuse.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:05.617
And so we just said
back to them, and said,

00:28:05.617 --> 00:28:07.140
"Our church did it."

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:10.447
Said, "If a church that doesn't
have the budget of a city,

00:28:10.447 --> 00:28:14.677
"cities have billions of
dollars, is able to do this,

00:28:14.677 --> 00:28:16.320
"why can't the city?"

00:28:16.320 --> 00:28:19.900
Long story short, they ended
up using the same principles

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:24.510
that we used, and I would invite anyone

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:28.320
who comes to Chicago to see
this crown jewel library now.

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:31.230
The Carter G. Woodson
Library is astonishing.

00:28:31.230 --> 00:28:34.410
It is one of the only libraries
in the city of Chicago

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:38.730
that specializes in
Alzheimer's and dementia,

00:28:38.730 --> 00:28:41.700
so if you're a caregiver,
you can go to this library.

00:28:41.700 --> 00:28:45.420
They have a studio in this library.

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:48.900
They have an amazing children's
section in this library.

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:51.090
There is an auditorium in this library.

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:52.800
You can have shows, and it's like,

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:57.630
it is truly a crown
jewel, and on top of that,

00:28:57.630 --> 00:28:59.760
you have the largest collection

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:02.940
of African American
literature in the Midwest,

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:07.940
and it's smack dab in the
heart of the South Side

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:10.285
in the Black community,

00:29:10.285 --> 00:29:14.820
and that's part of the
kind of development,

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:18.180
when I say community
development, is putting,

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:20.850
making sure that there is an institution

00:29:20.850 --> 00:29:23.190
that everyone can utilize,

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:26.880
and a library is one of those spaces

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:29.227
that nobody argues about.

00:29:29.227 --> 00:29:31.050
You know, you don't have, well,

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:32.340
well, down in Florida they're arguing

00:29:32.340 --> 00:29:35.040
about what's in the
libraries, another story,

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:38.340
but usually libraries are
the one community space

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:39.900
that just about everybody can agree on

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:41.956
that we wanna make sure
that it's functioning well.

00:29:41.956 --> 00:29:43.470
- Mm-hm.

00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:46.797
Wow, that's, I mean, just
transformative in so many ways.

00:29:46.797 --> 00:29:49.410
And just to know that
you all held your ground,

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:52.020
I mean there was a lot
of wisdom (chuckles)

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:54.960
in that negotiation
process I would call it,

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:58.590
but just showing the importance
of community as well.

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:00.030
I mean you touched on sustainability,

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:02.460
how has that played
out both in the library

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:03.660
and then what you all are doing just

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:05.310
in the community in general?

00:30:05.310 --> 00:30:06.720
- Oh it's been awesome,

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:11.100
so the next phase for the
Community Development Corporation,

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:14.340
they started purchasing
homes in the neighborhood

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:17.450
that were on the, what you would call the,

00:30:17.450 --> 00:30:19.860
part of the Cook County Land Bank.

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:23.550
They, tax delinquent homes.

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:26.370
You may get a home for 10,000
and they're falling apart,

00:30:26.370 --> 00:30:28.740
you know, 10,000, $15,000.

00:30:28.740 --> 00:30:33.740
And we had a developer who
was part of the church,

00:30:34.740 --> 00:30:37.470
and he wanted to develop the homes

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:41.970
so that they would look like
they did in the suburbs,

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:46.350
high end, high end, high end development.

00:30:46.350 --> 00:30:51.350
But we would not sell them
at anywhere at the cost

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:55.650
that they would valued at.

00:30:55.650 --> 00:30:56.820
And there was a reason.

00:30:56.820 --> 00:31:00.240
So let's say with the renovation,
they take, we get a home,

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:04.260
five, 10, 15, $30,000, whatever it maybe,

00:31:04.260 --> 00:31:08.553
and after everything is done
it's valued at, say, 175.

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:14.600
We sell it at 110, 105, or 95.

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:16.500
Why?

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:19.710
Because you walk in with equity.

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:23.880
So the moment you buy your
home, you walk in with equity,

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:28.650
and all of the kind of internal
appointments of the home,

00:31:28.650 --> 00:31:32.310
you know, the kitchen,
the lighting, you know,

00:31:32.310 --> 00:31:34.560
the whole nine, the way that the heating

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:36.660
and air cooling system is done,

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:40.140
would be on the same level if you drove

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:45.030
to one of the well-off suburbs,
so using the same framing,

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:49.110
so when the downturn happens, you know,

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:52.110
you've got equity in your
home, and if you need,

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:54.630
because one of the ways
that Black people make money

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:56.490
in terms of wealth, past wealth,

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:59.703
is we've really done that
through home ownership.

00:32:00.690 --> 00:32:02.280
So you've already, walking in,

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:07.280
you've got 25, $75,000 worth of equity.

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:11.010
That's only going to
increase, 10, 15, 20 years,

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:14.040
then you pass it on to your children

00:32:14.040 --> 00:32:17.040
and it does not become a
burden, it becomes an asset.

00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:18.300
So we're talking about, you know,

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:20.400
the idea of wealth building.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:22.140
So we start purchasing these homes

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:23.970
and then something else happened

00:32:23.970 --> 00:32:25.893
which we're dealing with now.

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:29.790
Then the speculators, these companies,

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:34.470
saw what we were doing and
they started grabbing the homes

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:38.850
on the tax delinquent
rolls in the Land Bank.

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:40.140
- Oh my goodness.

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:43.110
- And then trying to sell them without,

00:32:43.110 --> 00:32:44.578
not even close to any kind of,

00:32:44.578 --> 00:32:46.170
that means they're still falling apart,

00:32:46.170 --> 00:32:48.330
trying to sell them at a higher price

00:32:48.330 --> 00:32:50.300
or trying to sell 'em to us.

00:32:50.300 --> 00:32:53.245
"Oh yeah, we'll give
it to you for 80,000."

00:32:53.245 --> 00:32:54.078
I was like, "What're you talking about?

00:32:54.078 --> 00:32:55.290
"This thing was on the rolls for 10."

00:32:55.290 --> 00:32:56.123
That's a lot.

00:32:57.870 --> 00:32:59.190
It's not livable.

00:32:59.190 --> 00:33:01.500
You can't live in this
home, it's falling apart.

00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:06.500
And so we see how
capitalism ends up trying

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:12.200
to undermine our community
when these speculators come in

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:15.990
and are basically just trying
to, you know, make a buck,

00:33:15.990 --> 00:33:17.970
and we found out that all these companies,

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:18.803
all these individuals,

00:33:18.803 --> 00:33:23.690
and not a single one of them
lives in the city of Chicago.

00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:25.913
- Wow.

00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:28.823
Wow.

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:31.343
That was the one,

00:33:31.343 --> 00:33:32.670
I mean that was one of the
questions I was gonna ask

00:33:32.670 --> 00:33:35.310
of just, I was gonna ask
about pushback but this is,

00:33:35.310 --> 00:33:38.185
I mean that's a whole
nother level of just,

00:33:38.185 --> 00:33:40.050
people trying to, yeah.
- It's another kind of white,

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:40.953
yeah, nother kind of white supremacy.

00:33:40.953 --> 00:33:43.590
Here we have, we're doing
community rebuilding,

00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:46.590
we're doing what we're supposed
to do, we're doing the work,

00:33:46.590 --> 00:33:49.470
and then you have people literally outside

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:50.303
of the community say,

00:33:50.303 --> 00:33:52.357
"Well I'm just gonna make a profit off

00:33:52.357 --> 00:33:57.357
"of this working class,
this middle class community

00:33:57.367 --> 00:34:00.997
"in Chicago because there's
not gonna be anybody

00:34:00.997 --> 00:34:03.987
"that's gonna be an advocate for them."

00:34:05.070 --> 00:34:06.540
They got another thing
coming, but we still.

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:07.373
- Yeah.

00:34:07.373 --> 00:34:09.990
How do you maintain your hope

00:34:09.990 --> 00:34:11.760
and your ability to not be jaded?

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:13.500
'Cause I'm hearing you say
this for the first time

00:34:13.500 --> 00:34:16.800
and all sorts of reactions
are coming up inside me.

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:18.480
I'm just imagining how you
navigate that as a leader,

00:34:18.480 --> 00:34:20.040
and how do you keep people encourage

00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:22.067
in the midst of that?
- Well I'm, you know, I'm,

00:34:22.067 --> 00:34:23.310
I know this sounds funny,

00:34:23.310 --> 00:34:25.350
but I'm not trying to
be funny, but I'm Black.

00:34:25.350 --> 00:34:26.944
I mean, I mean.

00:34:26.944 --> 00:34:27.840
(both laugh)

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:28.743
- True.

00:34:28.743 --> 00:34:32.760
- I mean, when I, because
I'm a lover of history,

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:35.817
and I'm a history nerd,
I'm a Black history nerd.

00:34:35.817 --> 00:34:37.967
I'm into history, I'm
just nerd in general.

00:34:39.240 --> 00:34:40.187
But especially history nerd, and--

00:34:40.187 --> 00:34:41.484
- It's a good thing.

00:34:41.484 --> 00:34:45.150
- When I read, it is, it
is, nothing wrong, you know,

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:47.360
nerds rule the world
in that way, right now.

00:34:48.927 --> 00:34:53.927
But when I read stories of
Harriet Tubman, of Robert Small,

00:34:58.980 --> 00:35:02.220
of Ida B. Wells, I don't have the right,

00:35:02.220 --> 00:35:04.477
could you imagine Harriet Tubman saying,

00:35:04.477 --> 00:35:06.695
"You know, I'm feeling kinda jaded.

00:35:06.695 --> 00:35:10.320
"You know, I only got 300 out."

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:12.520
Like you got, do you
know what you just did?

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:16.560
You know, you were not only
a spy but you were also,

00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:17.430
you're going back

00:35:17.430 --> 00:35:20.880
and you're pulling these
people out of misery

00:35:20.880 --> 00:35:24.000
and emancipating them spiritually.

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:26.613
I always think that our
ancestors are saying to us,

00:35:27.870 --> 00:35:30.097
you know, "Otis, you
have all these resources

00:35:30.097 --> 00:35:33.217
"and communities that you
can connect with and people.

00:35:33.217 --> 00:35:36.813
"You have what your ancestors never had,

00:35:38.047 --> 00:35:43.047
"and you don't have the
right to be cynical."

00:35:43.940 --> 00:35:46.230
You have the right, you can get mad.

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:48.240
You have the right,
you can get ticked off,

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:51.933
and you can say the system
is rigged, but you don't,

00:35:52.830 --> 00:35:57.663
all that being Black is is an act of hope.

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:03.500
Being Black is an act of resistance.

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:05.910
Being Black in this country means

00:36:05.910 --> 00:36:10.910
that you are from the
descendants of people who refused

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:18.307
to give in to despair, and said,

00:36:18.307 --> 00:36:20.130
"I shall rise up again,"

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:21.090
and that's one of the beautiful things

00:36:21.090 --> 00:36:22.170
about the Black church.

00:36:22.170 --> 00:36:24.630
The Black church is not
the white church in,

00:36:24.630 --> 00:36:26.370
you know, Black face.

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:27.540
You know, we have our own theology.

00:36:27.540 --> 00:36:32.430
We have a theology that says,
that speaks about, you know,

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:37.430
I don't know, you know, when,
but I know it shall, you know,

00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:41.310
that we have this, it might
not come when you wanna,

00:36:41.310 --> 00:36:42.930
you know, but, and this was,

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:45.240
these were these acts of resistance.

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:49.176
We always place it in this
kind of ephemeral frame

00:36:49.176 --> 00:36:51.270
of Black people just, you know,

00:36:51.270 --> 00:36:53.430
some type of opioid or
something of that nature

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:56.280
for the masses, borrowing
from this, you know,

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:58.614
this Marxian idea, but in the reality,

00:36:58.614 --> 00:37:03.614
we were in the midst of
absurdity and tragedy.

00:37:04.610 --> 00:37:09.610
We were framing, creating
the scaffolding for hope,

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:15.810
and that hope was not
something that was out there.

00:37:15.810 --> 00:37:19.230
The hope was that I am a child of God,

00:37:19.230 --> 00:37:22.260
the hope was that I have the ability.

00:37:22.260 --> 00:37:23.670
That's what Frederick Douglass did.

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:27.240
He said, this was the
most articulate orator

00:37:27.240 --> 00:37:32.240
in the history of America,
who never went to school.

00:37:34.710 --> 00:37:38.040
Because he, there was one simple kernel

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:40.320
that Howard Thurman says.

00:37:40.320 --> 00:37:45.320
He said, "The moment that
enslaved Black people recognized

00:37:45.457 --> 00:37:47.347
"that they were loved,

00:37:47.347 --> 00:37:50.047
"and they were loved by something greater

00:37:50.047 --> 00:37:54.763
"than what was before them,
then resistance, resilience,

00:37:55.717 --> 00:38:00.420
"and revolution was always
a part of their spirit."

00:38:00.420 --> 00:38:01.253
- Mm.

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:05.673
So well said, I mean
just that perspective.

00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:10.350
I appreciate you just
highlighting that in so many ways,

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:11.880
I mean, in a lot of
ways it's just something

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:15.810
that we need to talk about
more and more and more,

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:16.980
because it's not,

00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:21.150
we don't all have the
Carter G. Woodson Library,

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:22.500
unfortunately.

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:23.730
That needs to be replicated.

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:24.577
That's a whole

00:38:24.577 --> 00:38:25.410
nother conversation.
- That's right,

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:26.721
everybody needs a Woodson Library, really,

00:38:26.721 --> 00:38:28.050
every community does.

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:29.520
It's a great library.

00:38:29.520 --> 00:38:30.900
- I mean, have there been conversations?

00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:32.520
I know you all are focused on, you know,

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:33.750
the work in the South Side of Chicago,

00:38:33.750 --> 00:38:37.770
but conversations on ways to
expand this to other areas

00:38:37.770 --> 00:38:39.090
or help replicate, I mean,

00:38:39.090 --> 00:38:40.440
just with colleagues that you have

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:42.360
in other locations?
- Yeah.

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:43.350
We've been even talking about, you know,

00:38:43.350 --> 00:38:47.400
maybe the possibility of doing
a conference, because we,

00:38:47.400 --> 00:38:48.923
it's not just the Woodson
Library in with the church.

00:38:48.923 --> 00:38:52.110
We have 27 acres that we've purchased,

00:38:52.110 --> 00:38:56.100
and we are developing
that, and that will be,

00:38:56.100 --> 00:39:00.150
already has a medical
center on the property.

00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:03.180
We're now doing the housing next.

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:06.750
It will eventually have an
urban farm, a five-acre farm,

00:39:06.750 --> 00:39:09.330
and it'll have a hotel that we're trying

00:39:09.330 --> 00:39:12.063
to do using the same
three principles, again,

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:16.260
Black folk gotta benefit,
gotta be sustainable,

00:39:16.260 --> 00:39:17.970
and those who are,

00:39:17.970 --> 00:39:19.770
gotta disrupt the mass
incarceration system.

00:39:19.770 --> 00:39:22.680
Those three principles
always have to be used

00:39:22.680 --> 00:39:23.880
in the development.

00:39:23.880 --> 00:39:25.590
And we're gonna have a healing garden

00:39:25.590 --> 00:39:27.330
in parts of the nature conservancy,

00:39:27.330 --> 00:39:29.760
and so it'll be over the next 10, 15,

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:30.960
it's gonna take a while to finish it

00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:33.420
but we've got phase one which was a,

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:34.890
and we're not talking about a free clinic,

00:39:34.890 --> 00:39:38.400
we're talking about primary
care, medical facility,

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:41.400
and get this, this
always trips people out,

00:39:41.400 --> 00:39:42.690
it's run by Black women.

00:39:42.690 --> 00:39:46.740
You know, it's four
brilliant, four brilliant,

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:51.740
and I say it again, brilliant
doctors who run this facility,

00:39:52.650 --> 00:39:57.570
and the facility is through
the largest healthcare provider

00:39:57.570 --> 00:39:59.370
in Illinois, Advocate Health Care.

00:39:59.370 --> 00:40:00.203
- Wow.

00:40:00.203 --> 00:40:03.213
- And so we developed the
land, they partner with us,

00:40:04.380 --> 00:40:06.000
and built the facility,

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:11.000
and it is a state-of-the-art
medical center, again,

00:40:11.370 --> 00:40:13.560
in the heart of the South Side.

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:15.900
It's not, you know, the, you know,

00:40:15.900 --> 00:40:17.490
there's nothing wrong with free clinics,

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:19.380
I'm not throwing shade there,

00:40:19.380 --> 00:40:21.300
but many times when we
think about our community,

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:23.130
we're thinking about something

00:40:23.130 --> 00:40:28.130
that is a federally subsidized clinic

00:40:30.030 --> 00:40:32.430
that is not doing primary care,

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:34.470
but it's also doing care for, you know,

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:36.870
for people obviously
who don't have access,

00:40:36.870 --> 00:40:39.330
but this is also
practicing preventive care,

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:42.060
so they send out nurses
- Mm, that's good.

00:40:42.060 --> 00:40:46.627
- into the community to let
them know, "Hey, you know,

00:40:46.627 --> 00:40:49.267
"can I come by your Big Wish meeting

00:40:49.267 --> 00:40:53.970
"and lemme show you signs for a stroke?"

00:40:53.970 --> 00:40:54.870
You know, because most of the time,

00:40:54.870 --> 00:40:56.670
we end up going to the emergency room

00:40:56.670 --> 00:40:59.100
for our healthcare instead
of doing the preventive work.

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:02.670
So the medical center is
working on preventive care,

00:41:02.670 --> 00:41:06.210
which is really exciting, so
we're trying to create a model,

00:41:06.210 --> 00:41:09.420
what does, what's the model
from cradle to the grave

00:41:09.420 --> 00:41:12.900
if you wanna create a community,
what do you need to do,

00:41:12.900 --> 00:41:15.303
and you know, church is kind of a heart.

00:41:16.440 --> 00:41:18.930
The library's another anchor,
right, in our community,

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:20.850
which is right down the
street, and then a mile down,

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:22.980
you have this 27 acres

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:26.820
that is next door to
Chicago State University,

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:31.800
which has become a predominantly
African American college.

00:41:31.800 --> 00:41:35.160
So we got a college,
a community, a church,

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:37.230
and the Carter G. Woodson Library,

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:40.710
all within a 2 1/2 mile radius,

00:41:40.710 --> 00:41:44.220
and now this main artery
known as 95th Street,

00:41:44.220 --> 00:41:48.930
we wanna develop that whole
street and not as gentrifying

00:41:48.930 --> 00:41:49.890
but as a renaissance,

00:41:49.890 --> 00:41:53.115
and we wanna be able to create
a model to say to everyone,

00:41:53.115 --> 00:41:54.390
this is what you can do
in your neighborhood,

00:41:54.390 --> 00:41:55.980
this is what you can do in your community,

00:41:55.980 --> 00:41:58.680
this is how you build, you know,

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:00.450
build communities so that children thrive,

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:02.310
so people are not getting shot.

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:07.230
This is the long-term, hardcore
work that you have to do.

00:42:07.230 --> 00:42:08.760
- Mm.

00:42:08.760 --> 00:42:11.730
Wow, that's just amazing to hear about,

00:42:11.730 --> 00:42:13.260
encouraging to hear about.

00:42:13.260 --> 00:42:15.000
I'm having a little
juxtaposition in my mind,

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:17.190
so just to give you a little
bit of insight, earlier,

00:42:17.190 --> 00:42:18.720
I guess a couple months
ago I was actually out

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:21.023
in Tulsa, Oklahoma. I
don't know if you know,

00:42:21.023 --> 00:42:23.790
but they opened a new museum out there

00:42:23.790 --> 00:42:26.580
on the 100-year anniversary
of the Tulsa Massacre.

00:42:26.580 --> 00:42:27.413
- Yes.

00:42:27.413 --> 00:42:29.010
- So to think about
everything that happened there

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:31.320
with Black Wall Street,
everything that changed,

00:42:31.320 --> 00:42:32.790
and to put that in juxtaposition

00:42:32.790 --> 00:42:35.610
with everything you all
are doing, it's just,

00:42:35.610 --> 00:42:36.840
I don't even know if I
can put it into words,

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:38.370
but just hearing, holding those two

00:42:38.370 --> 00:42:39.960
together is, yeah.
- The model, I mean,

00:42:39.960 --> 00:42:43.140
we're history nerds here,
so that's, you know,

00:42:43.140 --> 00:42:45.330
Tulsa is a model.

00:42:45.330 --> 00:42:47.520
But Auburn Avenue, I mean the history

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:49.680
of Auburn Avenue in Atlanta is,

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:50.940
there're these streets everywhere

00:42:50.940 --> 00:42:52.470
in the Black community that thrive.

00:42:52.470 --> 00:42:54.210
I mean, if you go to New Orleans,

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:57.990
if you were to go to Jackson,
if you went to Memphis,

00:42:57.990 --> 00:42:59.760
I mean every street,

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:03.270
there were like this five-block stretch,

00:43:03.270 --> 00:43:07.200
minimum five blocks, of I'm
not talking about, you know,

00:43:07.200 --> 00:43:09.630
little tiny little stores.

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:12.840
I'm talking about like
thriving businesses.

00:43:12.840 --> 00:43:17.840
Attorneys, doctors,
restaurants, dry cleaners,

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:20.010
every kinda business you
could kinda think of,

00:43:20.010 --> 00:43:22.830
and some places like in
Tulsa, they had hotels.

00:43:22.830 --> 00:43:24.510
You know, I mean it was amazing.

00:43:24.510 --> 00:43:27.420
Same thing with, on
Auburn Avenue in Atlanta,

00:43:27.420 --> 00:43:29.400
it had this history.

00:43:29.400 --> 00:43:31.560
So we wanna replicate that.

00:43:31.560 --> 00:43:33.780
You know, that there
was a model that worked,

00:43:33.780 --> 00:43:34.926
that was the model that worked,

00:43:34.926 --> 00:43:38.850
and when you build these institutions

00:43:38.850 --> 00:43:43.850
and you create the space
that allow people to thrive

00:43:44.400 --> 00:43:49.030
and we find out that home
ownership plus jobs plus churches

00:43:51.390 --> 00:43:56.390
that are engaged reduces
violence dramatically,

00:43:58.650 --> 00:44:03.650
then add gardens to the table,
plus libraries and schools,

00:44:06.300 --> 00:44:09.393
you then, the trend
goes down even farther.

00:44:10.230 --> 00:44:12.384
And so that's what we're trying to do.

00:44:12.384 --> 00:44:16.290
And then it's not rocket science
if you wanna change stuff,

00:44:16.290 --> 00:44:18.150
it's, we've been doing it for a long time.

00:44:18.150 --> 00:44:22.410
We know how to do it. We just
need to have the moral courage

00:44:22.410 --> 00:44:23.790
to be able to move forward.

00:44:23.790 --> 00:44:25.350
- Yeah.

00:44:25.350 --> 00:44:27.387
So well said, and I'll, I
mean just inspiring I know

00:44:27.387 --> 00:44:28.980
for a lot of our listeners,

00:44:28.980 --> 00:44:30.510
especially as we've been hinting

00:44:30.510 --> 00:44:33.663
at the fact that this history
is not readily taught.

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:36.450
Many people might be
hearing aspects of this

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:38.550
for the first time which I
think is also an important

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:39.383
and huge step.

00:44:39.383 --> 00:44:41.400
Obviously you all have
done a wonderful job

00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:42.630
of that already in your community,

00:44:42.630 --> 00:44:45.660
but as much as this can be modeled

00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:48.093
and expanded I think is so
important, so again, you know,

00:44:48.093 --> 00:44:50.640
it's just really encouraging
to hear about all

00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:54.030
that you're doing, in the midst,
in the midst of everything,

00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:55.650
even as you opened up this conversation,

00:44:55.650 --> 00:44:57.660
in the midst of everything
you're even experiencing

00:44:57.660 --> 00:45:00.930
in your pastoral role
day to day, so I mean,

00:45:00.930 --> 00:45:02.760
to me it's just deeply encouraging

00:45:02.760 --> 00:45:04.230
to know what you all are about,

00:45:04.230 --> 00:45:06.690
makes me wanna swing by at
some point just to get a,

00:45:06.690 --> 00:45:08.320
even a firsthand glimpse,
- I hope you would,

00:45:08.320 --> 00:45:09.153
we would love to have you.
- or get connected

00:45:09.153 --> 00:45:12.150
with some of those doctors, those women,

00:45:12.150 --> 00:45:14.310
Black women who are running things

00:45:14.310 --> 00:45:17.730
and doing so, so
brilliantly, it's just, yeah.

00:45:17.730 --> 00:45:19.230
I'm speechless but I'm talking a lot.

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:21.300
It's a mixture of the
combination of both coming out

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:24.030
at once, but any last words

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:26.280
that you wanna leave our
listeners with in terms

00:45:26.280 --> 00:45:29.283
of either word of encouragement
or a word of challenge?

00:45:31.260 --> 00:45:35.520
- I would say that you are birthed

00:45:35.520 --> 00:45:40.520
and born with the power of
resilience and resistance.

00:45:42.090 --> 00:45:44.088
Just needs to be nurtured.

00:45:44.088 --> 00:45:49.088
Find spaces, find people
who will pour into you,

00:45:50.310 --> 00:45:53.790
and when they pour into you,

00:45:53.790 --> 00:45:56.280
make sure you pour into somebody else.

00:45:56.280 --> 00:45:59.790
There's nothing more
dangerous than a person

00:45:59.790 --> 00:46:04.790
who acts like a swamp instead
of acting like a canal.

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:08.490
Swamps have inlets with no outlet,

00:46:08.490 --> 00:46:12.300
and that's why swamps
smell and things die.

00:46:12.300 --> 00:46:15.330
But canals are designed to release

00:46:15.330 --> 00:46:16.803
for those who are in need.

00:46:17.700 --> 00:46:19.650
And so be a canal.

00:46:19.650 --> 00:46:23.820
When someone pours into you,
that's all we're called to do.

00:46:23.820 --> 00:46:25.140
Just don't be a swamp.

00:46:25.140 --> 00:46:26.940
That's what America wants you to be.

00:46:28.260 --> 00:46:30.000
- Amen to that.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:31.200
Well again Dr. Moss,

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:33.423
just appreciate you taking
the time for everything

00:46:33.423 --> 00:46:35.640
that you shared, all the
work that you all are doing

00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:36.930
in community together.

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:39.480
So grateful and honored to be
able to host you here today.

00:46:39.480 --> 00:46:40.770
Thank you again.

00:46:40.770 --> 00:46:41.730
- Oh, it's my pleasure,

00:46:41.730 --> 00:46:44.490
and we look forward to
seeing you in Chicago

00:46:44.490 --> 00:46:45.360
some time.
- Yep,

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:46.714
that would be wonderful.

00:46:46.714 --> 00:46:49.381
